{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"id\":\"R-2:110\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"The option to take goodness among the people in 18:86 gains a possible action-and-reward frame from 2:110, where good is sent ahead and found with God; 18:88 then speaks of the reward of al-ḥusnā. This is a broad association, not an equation of these acts.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"أَن تَتَّخِذَ فِيهِمْ حُسْنًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"2:110\",\"ar\":\"وَمَا تُقَدِّمُوا۟ لِأَنفُسِكُم مِّنْ خَيْرٍۢ تَجِدُوهُ عِندَ ٱللَّهِ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:88\",\"ar\":\"فَلَهُۥ جَزَآءً ٱلْحُسْنَىٰ\"}],\"activation\":\"The good-treatment branch is taken up immediately afterward as a reward for faith and righteous action.\",\"limits\":\"2:110 uses خَيْر, while the focus has حُسْنًا; neither verse explicitly says that DQ’s treatment earns this reward.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-2:190\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"The ban on transgression in 2:190 could constrain how the punishment option in 18:86 is read if that punishment involves force against aggressors.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"إِمَّآ أَن تُعَذِّبَ\"},{\"ref\":\"2:190\",\"ar\":\"وَقَٰتِلُوا۟ فِى سَبِيلِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلَّذِينَ يُقَٰتِلُونَكُمْ وَلَا تَعْتَدُوا۟\"}],\"missing\":\"The focus and its continuation do not say that these people fought or that تُعَذِّبَ means armed combat; evidence connecting the people or this punishment to combat would make the restraint directly applicable.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-2:196\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-2:231\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-2:234\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-2:235\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-2:247\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The preceding account says God established DQ in the land and gave him means; 2:247 likewise presents Talut’s kingship and capacities as God’s choice and gift. This places the discretion offered in 18:86 within a pattern of divinely enabled rule.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"18:84\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّا مَكَّنَّا لَهُۥ فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ وَءَاتَيْنَٰهُ مِن كُلِّ شَىْءٍۢ سَبَبًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"2:247\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ ٱصْطَفَىٰهُ عَلَيْكُمْ وَزَادَهُۥ بَسْطَةًۭ فِى ٱلْعِلْمِ وَٱلْجِسْمِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus follows the statement of DQ’s empowerment with God’s direct address giving him alternatives for a people.\",\"limits\":\"The parallel does not identify DQ with Talut or establish that their offices and responsibilities were identical.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-2:251\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"David’s victory by God’s leave and receipt of kingship and wisdom are followed by a statement that God checks people through one another to prevent corruption in the land. This offers a public-order frame alongside DQ’s choices concerning a people.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"إِمَّآ أَن تُعَذِّبَ وَإِمَّآ أَن تَتَّخِذَ فِيهِمْ حُسْنًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"2:251\",\"ar\":\"وَءَاتَىٰهُ ٱللَّهُ ٱلْمُلْكَ وَٱلْحِكْمَةَ وَعَلَّمَهُۥ مِمَّا يَشَآءُ ۗ وَلَوْلَا دَفْعُ ٱللَّهِ ٱلنَّاسَ بَعْضَهُم بِبَعْضٍۢ لَّفَسَدَتِ ٱلْأَرْضُ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus presents DQ’s power as a choice over how to deal with people, after the surah has described his empowerment.\",\"limits\":\"The passage does not say that DQ’s punishment option serves the same anti-corruption purpose stated in 2:251.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-2:283\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-3:37\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"The repeated phrase وَجَدَ عِندَهَا makes both discoveries location-bound, but the objects differ: DQ finds people, while Maryam finds provision. It offers a small narrative-syntax echo without transferring the meaning of either scene.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"وَوَجَدَ عِندَهَا قَوْمًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"3:37\",\"ar\":\"وَجَدَ عِندَهَا رِزْقًۭا\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus uses the same finding-and-location collocation for the encounter that follows DQ’s observation of the setting sun.\",\"limits\":\"The parallel does not identify the feminine referent in 18:86 or imply that the people are provision or a divine gift.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-3:40\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-3:45\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-3:65\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-4:12\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-4:52\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-4:54\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"The great kingdom given to Abraham’s family provides another broad instance of political power described as God’s gift, alongside the account of DQ’s establishment in the land.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"18:84\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّا مَكَّنَّا لَهُۥ فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ\"},{\"ref\":\"4:54\",\"ar\":\"فَقَدْ ءَاتَيْنَآ ءَالَ إِبْرَٰهِيمَ ٱلْكِتَٰبَ وَٱلْحِكْمَةَ وَءَاتَيْنَٰهُم مُّلْكًا عَظِيمًۭا\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus’s choice over a people follows a statement that God enabled DQ and gave him means.\",\"limits\":\"4:54 concerns a charge of envy and does not describe DQ’s office or the proper use of his power.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-4:58\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The focus gives DQ discretion over a people; 4:58 names returning trusts and judging people with justice as duties of authority. It supplies an explicit Qur’anic ethical frame for that discretion.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"قُلْنَا يَٰذَا ٱلْقَرْنَيْنِ إِمَّآ أَن تُعَذِّبَ وَإِمَّآ أَن تَتَّخِذَ فِيهِمْ حُسْنًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"4:58\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ يَأْمُرُكُمْ أَن تُؤَدُّوا۟ ٱلْأَمَٰنَٰتِ إِلَىٰٓ أَهْلِهَا وَإِذَا حَكَمْتُم بَيْنَ ٱلنَّاسِ أَن تَحْكُمُوا۟ بِٱلْعَدْلِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus has God address DQ about what to do with people he has encountered; 4:58 explicitly speaks of judging among people.\",\"limits\":\"4:58 does not say which option in 18:86 is just or define the standard for choosing between them.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-4:64\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"The focus depicts DQ receiving a direct divine address, while 4:64 says messengers are sent to be obeyed by God’s permission; the comparison would matter if DQ’s role were established as that of a messenger.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"قُلْنَا يَٰذَا ٱلْقَرْنَيْنِ\"},{\"ref\":\"4:64\",\"ar\":\"وَمَآ أَرْسَلْنَا مِن رَّسُولٍ إِلَّا لِيُطَاعَ بِإِذْنِ ٱللَّهِ\"}],\"missing\":\"The focus does not call DQ a messenger. An explicit designation of his role in this surah or another Qur’anic passage could establish whether the messenger-authority parallel applies.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-4:82\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-4:88\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-4:94\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"The command to verify before judging someone’s belief in 4:94 could serve as a caution when applying DQ’s punishment option, especially since 18:87–88 distinguish people by wrongdoing or faith and righteous action.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"إِمَّآ أَن تُعَذِّبَ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:87\",\"ar\":\"أَمَّا مَن ظَلَمَ فَسَوْفَ نُعَذِّبُهُ\"},{\"ref\":\"4:94\",\"ar\":\"فَتَبَيَّنُوا۟ وَلَا تَقُولُوا۟ لِمَنْ أَلْقَىٰٓ إِلَيْكُمُ ٱلسَّلَٰمَ لَسْتَ مُؤْمِنًۭا\"}],\"missing\":\"The focus does not describe combat, a greeting, or uncertainty about belief; 4:94 addresses those circumstances. Evidence that DQ’s people faced an analogous status judgment would make the caution more than a general analogy.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-4:102\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-4:110\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The focus’s punishment branch is followed by the condition مَن ظَلَمَ; 4:110 uses the same condition for someone who then seeks forgiveness and finds God forgiving. This leaves open repentance as a further path for a wrongdoer, absent from DQ’s stated options.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"إِمَّآ أَن تُعَذِّبَ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:87\",\"ar\":\"أَمَّا مَن ظَلَمَ فَسَوْفَ نُعَذِّبُهُۥ ثُمَّ يُرَدُّ إِلَىٰ رَبِّهِۦ فَيُعَذِّبُهُۥ عَذَابًۭا نُّكْرًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"4:110\",\"ar\":\"وَمَن يَعْمَلْ سُوٓءًا أَوْ يَظْلِمْ نَفْسَهُۥ ثُمَّ يَسْتَغْفِرِ ٱللَّهَ يَجِدِ ٱللَّهَ غَفُورًۭا رَّحِيمًۭا\"}],\"activation\":\"Both passages connect wrongdoing with a consequence, but 4:110 adds seeking forgiveness after the wrong.\",\"limits\":\"4:110 does not say that DQ’s immediate punishment is waived; the repentant condition is not stated in 18:87–88.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"X1\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The immediate continuation specifies the two branches: 18:87 ties punishment to wrongdoing, while 18:88 connects faith and righteous action with the reward of al-ḥusnā and ease. The options thus receive moral conditions and consequences in the narrative that follows.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"إِمَّآ أَن تُعَذِّبَ وَإِمَّآ أَن تَتَّخِذَ فِيهِمْ حُسْنًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"18:87\",\"ar\":\"أَمَّا مَن ظَلَمَ فَسَوْفَ نُعَذِّبُهُ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:88\",\"ar\":\"وَأَمَّا مَنْ ءَامَنَ وَعَمِلَ صَٰلِحًۭا فَلَهُۥ جَزَآءً ٱلْحُسْنَىٰ ۖ وَسَنَقُولُ لَهُۥ مِنْ أَمْرِنَا يُسْرًۭا\"}],\"activation\":\"These are the next two verses after the offered alternatives, and each begins by taking up one branch with أَمَّا.\",\"limits\":\"The wording distinguishes DQ’s announced punishment from the later return to the Lord and divine punishment; those consequences should not be collapsed into one act.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"X2\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The sunset endpoint begins a repeated journey-and-finding pattern: at sunrise DQ reaches another limit and finds a people, then at the passes he finds a third people who address him directly. The narrative moves from an offered choice about the first people to a community asking him for protection from corruption.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"18:86\",\"ar\":\"حَتَّىٰٓ إِذَا بَلَغَ مَغْرِبَ ٱلشَّمْسِ وَجَدَهَا تَغْرُبُ فِى عَيْنٍ حَمِئَةٍۢ وَوَجَدَ عِندَهَا قَوْمًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"18:90\",\"ar\":\"حَتَّىٰٓ إِذَا بَلَغَ مَطْلِعَ ٱلشَّمْسِ وَجَدَهَا تَطْلُعُ عَلَىٰ قَوْمٍۢ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:93\",\"ar\":\"حَتَّىٰٓ إِذَا بَلَغَ بَيْنَ ٱلسَّدَّيْنِ وَجَدَ مِن دُونِهِمَا قَوْمًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"18:94\",\"ar\":\"قَالُوا۟ يَٰذَا ٱلْقَرْنَيْنِ إِنَّ يَأْجُوجَ وَمَأْجُوجَ مُفْسِدُونَ فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The repeated بلغ and وجد formulas connect the focus’s western endpoint to the eastern journey and the later encounter at the passes.\",\"limits\":\"The formal parallel does not by itself settle how to interpret the horizon descriptions or establish that the three communities have the same circumstances.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}"}}
